go-to: dingeldey

@dingeldey
on-arrival: gold_leader = "Coalfax"; guild_gold -= 2; burgher_gold -= 2; guild_royalist += 2; burgher_royalist += 2; pro_republic -= 3; liberal_cooperation -= 1; bourgeois_cooperation += 1
face-image: img/portraits/dingeldey.jpg

Coalfax's election to the chairmanship represents a triumph for the reform-minded elements on the <span style="color: #C0A054;">**Golds**</span>'s left wing and met with strong approval from the movement towards propertied unity. However, despite the enthusiasm exhibited by those on the left wing, his election doesn't seem to represent a shift to the left at all and only confirms the party's shift to the right. [? if chancellor == "Askew" and not marcher_in_government: Coalfax doesn't even feel great loyalty toward the Askew government and believes that the party could only fulfill its mission towards propertied concentration after it is freed from the burden of governmental responsibility.?] So far, he has proven to be a weak and ineffectual leader, capitulating to the right wing at every possible opportunity.

Furthermore, Coalfax is not particularly interested in a closer relationship with the <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**[+ ddp_name +]**</span> and definitely not with us, but rather with parties to the right, such as the <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span> and even the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>, to transform them into a 'constructive component of the state.' This does not bode well for the future of Unity democracy.